detail.abstract:
Introduction: Labour migration is central to Southeast Asia's political economy, yet regional protection for migrant workers remains weakly enforceable. Existing accounts blame noninterference and consensus decision-making; this article asks what that explanation leaves out.&nbsp;
<div>
 Methods: Qualitative documentary analysis of Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) instruments (the 2007 Declaration, 2015 trafficking convention, 2017 Consensus and 2024 portability Guidelines), the negotiating record of the committee implementing the 2007 Declaration, and International Labour Organization (ILO) and United Nations (UN) standards.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results: Documentary evidence points to three mechanisms behind protection without enforcement: sovereignty-preserving drafting, sending-receiving bargaining over bindingness, and localisation of imprecise norms. The privileging of skilled over low-skilled mobility is consistent with the same mechanisms.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Conclusion: Intermediate, non-adjudicatory mechanisms, such as comparable indicators and claim-preservation procedures, offer a more realistic route to accountability than a supranational court, under identifiable conditions.
</div>
